   "text": "This SMILES was read from the drawing in three parts, and the stereochemistry is assumed to match lovastatin rather than checked wedge by wedge.\n\n1. **Left end:** a glutarimide (2,6-dioxopiperidin-3-yl) is attached to the nitrogen of a phthalimide. The phthalimide carries an NH at the 4-position of its benzene ring.\n2. **Linker:** the NH is followed by CH2CH2\u2013O\u2013CH2CH2\u2013O\u2013CH2CH2\u2013NH, which ends in a carbamate, NH\u2013C(=O)\u2013O.\n3. **Right end:** the carbamate oxygen sits on C1 of a hexahydronaphthalene. This ring system has a methyl on the CH next to the ring CH2 and a second methyl on the other ring, with two ring C=C bonds. The ring carries a CH2CH2 chain to a six-membered lactone, and the lactone bears an O-benzyl ether at the 4-position. This is the lovastatin skeleton, with the 2-methylbutanoyl ester replaced by the carbamate and the lactone OH replaced by OBn.\n\nI took the stereocentres from lovastatin's known SMILES. Swapping OH for OBn and the acyl group for the carbamate does not change the neighbour order at any stereocentre, so the @/@@ tags carry over unchanged.\n\nSMILES: O=C1CCC(N2C(=O)c3cccc(NCCOCCOCCNC(=O)O[C@H]4C[C@H](C=C5[C@H]4[C@H]([C@H](C=C5)C)CC[C@@H]6C[C@H](CC(=O)O6)OCc7ccccc7)C)c3C2=O)C(=O)N1"
  }
